# {{ title }}

Export any `.yamd` file to HTML, PDF, Word or a slide deck — all from the command palette, with no sign-in or server required.

::toc{depth=2}

---

## Export as HTML

**Command:** YAMD: Export as HTML

Produces a self-contained `.html` file next to the note. It includes:

- A **title page** with the title, author, course and date from the front matter (shown when `title:` is set)
- A **linked table of contents** (shown when there are two or more headings)
- Syntax-highlighted code blocks
- KaTeX-rendered math, inlined into the HTML so it works offline
- A print stylesheet for clean printing

The HTML file is self-contained — fonts, styles and math are all embedded.
Share it as a single file or open it in any browser.

---

## Export as PDF

**Command:** YAMD: Export as PDF

Runs **Export as HTML** first, then opens the result in your system browser.
Use the browser's **File → Print → Save as PDF** (or :kbd[Cmd+P] on Mac,
:kbd[Ctrl+P] on Windows/Linux) to save the PDF.

The print stylesheet hides the export banner and adds page breaks after the
title page and at appropriate points in the content.

:::tip {title="Tip: page numbers"}
Chrome and Edge include page numbers in the header/footer by default.
Uncheck them in the print dialog if you don't want them.
:::

---

## Export as Word (.docx)

**Command:** YAMD: Export as Word (.docx)

Produces a `.docx` file next to the note. Supported content:

| YAMD | Word equivalent |
| ---- | --------------- |
| Headings 1–6 | Heading 1–6 styles |
| Paragraphs | Normal style |
| **Bold**, *italic*, `code` | Character formatting |
| [Links](https://example.com) | Hyperlinks |
| Tables | Word tables |
| Task lists | Checkbox items (tick character) |
| Block quotes | Indented paragraph |
| Horizontal rules | Page break |

Math, images and complex directives are replaced with placeholder text in the
current version.

---

## Export as Slides (HTML)

**Command:** YAMD: Export as Slides (HTML)

Turns the note into a keyboard-navigable HTML presentation. Each **level-1 heading** starts a new slide. Alternatively, put a `---` thematic break between slides if you want multiple slides without top-level headings.

Navigate with :kbd[←] / :kbd[→] or :kbd[PageUp] / :kbd[PageDown]. A slide
counter at the bottom shows your position.

The slide view respects the print stylesheet: :kbd[Cmd+P] / :kbd[Ctrl+P] prints
one slide per page.

### Structuring a slide deck

```yamd
---
title: CS201 — Binary Search Trees
author: Ada Lovelace
date: 2026-10-17
course: CS201
---

# CS201 — Binary Search Trees

A 25-minute lecture.

# What is a BST?

A binary tree where every left child is smaller
and every right child is larger.

:::definition {title="Binary search tree" #def:bst}
…
:::

# Operations

- **Search** — $O(h)$
- **Insert** — $O(h)$
- **Delete** — $O(h)$

# Questions?
```

---

## Where the files are saved

All export commands save the output file **in the same folder as the note**,
using the note's file name with a different extension:

| Command | Output file |
| ------- | ----------- |
| Export as HTML | `notename.html` |
| Export as Slides | `notename-slides.html` |
| Export as Word | `notename.docx` |

A notification appears when the file is saved, with the file name.
